#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devloop-auto-mode-question: whether the question on the rule for the
classifier of auto mode is put at a start with --auto, and the record of the
refusals that bring it.

Since 22 October 2026, the roadmap entry of that date on the question after a
refusal of auto mode. The question of text 4 of the roadmap entry of 9 October
2026 - may devloop enter the rule for the classifier into the person's
settings - is put at a first setup after the yes to the unattended mode, in
setup-checks step 8, and otherwise only at a start with --auto, with the
person there, where two things hold together: the rule does not stand in
their settings, or the settings cannot be read for it, and the project
carries an open issue for an install that auto mode refused in a run without
them. Nothing is stored on a no: while such an issue is open the question
comes at every start with --auto, and once the issue is closed it ends with
no further step. A refusal counts where auto mode refused, with a verdict of
its classifier, a command the install guard reads as an install by one of
the ways the rule names - brew install, go install, cargo install, uv tool
install, pipx install, playwright install, cypress install, puppeteer
browsers install - and nothing on the command stays the person's under every
answer; hooks/pre-tool-use-install-guard.sh decides that at the event
PermissionDenied with its own reading and records it through this program.
The rule can stand in the person's settings alone: the page "Configure auto
mode" of Claude Code, read on 22 October 2026, says the classifier does not
read autoMode from a project's .claude/settings.json or settings.local.json.

Usage
    devloop-auto-mode-question [DIR]
        decides the question. Exit 0 where it is to be put, with the lines
        below; 1 where it is not; 2 where it could not be decided - gh did not
        answer, printed no JSON, or the rule program could not be run - with
        the command and the message, so that the run says so, as "When a
        command does not answer" asks, and concludes nothing.
    devloop-auto-mode-question record WAY ID [DIR]   < the command
        records a refusal, from the install guard at PermissionDenied and
        from nowhere else: WAY the way of the rule the command took, ID the
        tool call's id, the command on stdin as the JSON string literal
        bin/devloop-command-reader prints on its command: line, one line.
        Exit 0 where the line stands afterwards, 2 where it could not be
        written, with the reason.
    devloop-auto-mode-question --self-test

DIR is the project directory; without it CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR, and without that
the current directory, as bin/devloop-install-record takes it.

Where the record stands. One file, devloop-auto-mode-denials, in the git
directory the project's worktrees share - git rev-parse --git-common-dir,
.git of the project in the ordinary case - so that it stands inside the
project, never in its tree and never in a commit, and nobody has to know of
it or delete it: a line that no open issue matches does nothing, and the
question ends with the issue. One line per refusal: the time in UTC, the
way, the call's id and the command as the JSON carried it, separated by
tabs. The path and the form of the line live here and nowhere else.

What decides the question. First the rule, bin/devloop-auto-mode-rule read
beside this program, run with the interpreter running this one: exit 0, the
rule stands, ends it with 1, and gh is not called. Then the record: no git
directory, no file or no line ends it with 1, and gh is not called. Then the
open issues of the project labelled raised-here and needs-human, read with
gh issue list, and each recorded command held against the title and the body
of each issue: an issue that carries the command as the tool ran it,
verbatim, is one the question names - the skills have every such issue carry
the command as it ran, so the match is mechanical and the run's judgement
decides nothing here; an issue that carries it otherwise is not found, and
the question is not put, which is the side "In doubt, a run with nobody
there is not blocked" in docs/skill-conventions.md reads. For each issue
found, its kind: it holds a task where an open issue is blocked by it, read
with gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/N/dependencies/blocking, the
relation the readiness query of build-work reads from the other side, and
it concerns a check class otherwise, since such an issue is recorded as a
blocker of no task. gh is called in DIR, as the skills call it, each call
with 60 seconds.

On exit 0, one line per key, in this order:
    question: yes
    read: 1|2             the exit of bin/devloop-auto-mode-rule read
    rule: <its line>
    issue: <number>
    kind: task|class
    title: <title>
    way: <the way of the rule the command took>
    command: <the command as it ran, a line break printed as one space>
the five lines of an issue repeated per issue, in the order of their numbers,
each issue once. On exit 1 the first line reads question: no, the second
read: 0|1|2, the third rule: <the line of read>, and a fourth says why where
the rule did not end it: denials: none recorded, or issues: none ... carries
a recorded command. On exit 2 the first line reads question: not decided,
then command: <the command that did not answer> and message: <what came
back>. record prints one line, recorded in <path>: <way>, or not recorded:
<reason>.

It needs python3, git and gh, and nothing else. The self-test runs every
outcome against a stand-in for gh that answers from scripted files, as the
self-test of bin/devloop-arm does: the stand-in comes first on the path, the
configuration of gh points at an empty directory, no token stands in the
environment, and nothing of it reaches GitHub; the settings of this machine
are not touched, CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR pointing under the test's own directory.
The stand-in is a shell script, which a process started from Python finds
on macOS and Linux and not on Windows, so the self-test refuses there with
exit 2 and the workflow does not run it on that system; the program itself
runs there, gh being found as gh.exe.
"""

import datetime
import json
import os
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
RULE = os.path.join(HERE, "devloop-auto-mode-rule")
FILE = "devloop-auto-mode-denials"
LABELS = ["raised-here", "needs-human"]
SECONDS = 60


def usage(msg):
    print("devloop-auto-mode-question: %s" % msg, file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


def project_dir(given):
    return given or os.environ.get("CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR") or os.getcwd()


def common_dir(d):
    """The git directory the project's worktrees share, absolute, or None
    with the reason."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", d, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute",
                            "--git-common-dir"], capture_output=True, text=True)
    except OSError as e:
        return None, "git could not be run: %s" % e
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None, (r.stderr.strip() or r.stdout.strip() or
                      "git rev-parse ended with %d" % r.returncode)
    return r.stdout.strip(), None


def one_line(s):
    return " ".join(s.replace("\r\n", "\n").replace("\r", "\n").split("\n"))


def flat(s):
    """A field of the record on one line: every tab and line break a space."""
    return " ".join(s.split())


def record(way, call_id, d):
    literal = sys.stdin.read()
    literal = literal.split("\n", 1)[0].rstrip("\r")
    try:
        json.loads('"' + literal + '"')
    except ValueError:
        print("not recorded: the command on stdin is no JSON string literal")
        return 2
    cd, err = common_dir(d)
    if cd is None:
        print("not recorded: %s is no git repository: %s" % (d, err))
        return 2
    path = os.path.join(cd, FILE)
    now = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
    line = "%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n" % (now, flat(way), flat(call_id), literal)
    try:
        with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(line)
    except OSError as e:
        print("not recorded: %s" % e)
        return 2
    print("recorded in %s: %s" % (path, flat(way)))
    return 0


def denials(path):
    """The recorded refusals: (time, way, id, command), in order; a line not
    in the form is read over."""
    out = []
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        return out
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        for line in f:
            parts = line.rstrip("\n").split("\t", 3)
            if len(parts) != 4:
                continue
            try:
                cmd = json.loads('"' + parts[3] + '"')
            except ValueError:
                continue
            if cmd:
                out.append((parts[0], parts[1], parts[2], cmd))
    return out


def gh(args, d):
    """The output of gh, or None with the command and the message."""
    shown = "gh " + " ".join(args)
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["gh"] + args, cwd=d, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=SECONDS)
    except OSError as e:
        return None, shown, str(e)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return None, shown, "no answer within %d seconds" % SECONDS
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None, shown, (r.stderr.strip() or r.stdout.strip() or
                             "exit %d with nothing printed" % r.returncode)
    return r.stdout, shown, None


def not_decided(command, message):
    print("question: not decided")
    print("command: %s" % one_line(command))
    print("message: %s" % one_line(message))
    return 2


def question(d):
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, RULE, "read"], capture_output=True,
                           text=True)
    except OSError as e:
        return not_decided("%s read" % RULE, str(e))
    rule_line = one_line(r.stdout.strip() or r.stderr.strip())
    if r.returncode == 0:
        print("question: no")
        print("read: 0")
        print("rule: %s" % rule_line)
        return 1
    if r.returncode not in (1, 2):
        return not_decided("%s read" % RULE,
                           "ended with %d: %s" % (r.returncode, rule_line))
    head = ["read: %d" % r.returncode, "rule: %s" % rule_line]

    def no(why):
        print("question: no")
        for h in head:
            print(h)
        print(why)
        return 1

    cd, err = common_dir(d)
    if cd is None:
        return no("denials: none can stand, %s is no git repository: %s"
                  % (d, one_line(err)))
    recorded = denials(os.path.join(cd, FILE))
    if not recorded:
        return no("denials: none recorded")
    out, shown, err = gh(["issue", "list", "--state", "open",
                          "--label", LABELS[0], "--label", LABELS[1],
                          "--limit", "100", "--json", "number,title,body"], d)
    if out is None:
        return not_decided(shown, err)
    try:
        issues = json.loads(out)
        if not isinstance(issues, list):
            raise ValueError("not a list")
    except ValueError as e:
        return not_decided(shown, "printed no JSON list of issues: %s" % e)
    found = []
    for issue in sorted((i for i in issues if isinstance(i, dict)),
                        key=lambda i: i.get("number") or 0):
        number = issue.get("number")
        if not isinstance(number, int):
            continue
        text = (issue.get("title") or "") + "\n" + (issue.get("body") or "")
        for _, way, _, cmd in recorded:
            if cmd in text:
                found.append((number, issue.get("title") or "", way, cmd))
                break
    if not found:
        return no("issues: none of the %d open issues labelled %s and %s "
                  "carries a recorded command" % (len(issues), LABELS[0], LABELS[1]))
    lines = []
    for number, title, way, cmd in found:
        out, shown, err = gh(["api", "repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/%d/dependencies/blocking"
                              % number], d)
        if out is None:
            return not_decided(shown, err)
        try:
            blocking = json.loads(out)
            if not isinstance(blocking, list):
                raise ValueError("not a list")
        except ValueError as e:
            return not_decided(shown, "printed no JSON list of issues: %s" % e)
        kind = "task" if any(isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("state") == "open"
                             for b in blocking) else "class"
        lines += ["issue: %d" % number, "kind: %s" % kind,
                  "title: %s" % one_line(title), "way: %s" % way,
                  "command: %s" % one_line(cmd)]
    print("question: yes")
    for h in head:
        print(h)
    for line in lines:
        print(line)
    return 0


# ------------------------------------------------------------- the self-test

STAND_IN = r'''#!/bin/sh
# the stand-in for gh of the self-test of devloop-auto-mode-question: answers
# the issue list and the blocking relation of one issue from scripted files
# under $DEVLOOP_QUESTION_SCRIPT, logs every call, and marks a call no answer
# was scripted for
S="$DEVLOOP_QUESTION_SCRIPT"
echo "$*" >> "$S/log"
case "$*" in
  "issue list "*) a="$S/issues" ;;
  "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/"*"/dependencies/blocking") n=$(printf '%s' "$2" | sed 's#.*/issues/\([0-9]*\)/.*#\1#'); a="$S/blocking.$n" ;;
  *) a="$S/other" ;;
esac
[ -e "$a.rc" ] || { echo "stand-in for gh: no answer scripted for: gh $*" >&2; : > "$S/unscripted"; exit 1; }
cat "$a.out"; cat "$a.err" >&2
exit "$(cat "$a.rc")"
'''


def selftest():
    if os.name == "nt":
        print("devloop-auto-mode-question: the self-test does not run on "
              "Windows, where a process started from Python does not find a "
              "shell script as gh", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    me = os.path.realpath(__file__)
    # the real path, since /var is a link to /private/var on macOS
    T = os.path.realpath(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="devloop-auto-mode-question."))
    n = [0]
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "bin"))
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "gh-config"))
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "config"))
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "s"))
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "p"))
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, "nogit"))
        gh_path = os.path.join(T, "bin", "gh")
        with open(gh_path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(STAND_IN)
        os.chmod(gh_path, os.stat(gh_path).st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
        subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", os.path.join(T, "p")], check=True)
        env = dict(os.environ)
        for k in ("GH_TOKEN", "GITHUB_TOKEN", "GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN",
                  "GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN", "GH_HOST", "CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR"):
            env.pop(k, None)
        env["PATH"] = os.path.join(T, "bin") + os.pathsep + env.get("PATH", "")
        env["GH_CONFIG_DIR"] = os.path.join(T, "gh-config")
        env["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = os.path.join(T, "config")
        env["DEVLOOP_QUESTION_SCRIPT"] = os.path.join(T, "s")
        found = shutil.which("gh", path=env["PATH"])
        if found != gh_path:
            print("FAIL the stand-in for gh is not the gh the path finds: %s" % found)
            return 1
        S = os.path.join(T, "s")
        settings = os.path.join(T, "config", "settings.json")
        denials_file = os.path.join(T, "p", ".git", FILE)

        def fresh():
            for name in os.listdir(S):
                os.unlink(os.path.join(S, name))
            open(os.path.join(S, "log"), "w").close()
            if os.path.exists(settings):
                os.unlink(settings)
            if os.path.exists(denials_file):
                os.unlink(denials_file)

        def ans(kind, rc, out, err=""):
            for suffix, content in ((".rc", "%d\n" % rc), (".out", out), (".err", err)):
                with open(os.path.join(S, kind + suffix), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    f.write(content)

        def issues(*items):
            ans("issues", 0, json.dumps([{"number": num, "title": title, "body": body}
                                         for num, title, body in items]))

        def blocking(num, *states):
            ans("blocking.%d" % num, 0, json.dumps(
                [{"number": 900 + i, "state": s} for i, s in enumerate(states)]))

        def run(*args, stdin=""):
            r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me] + list(args), cwd=os.path.join(T, "p"),
                               input=stdin, capture_output=True, text=True, env=env)
            return r.returncode, r.stdout + r.stderr

        def calls():
            with open(os.path.join(S, "log"), encoding="utf-8") as f:
                return [l.strip() for l in f if l.strip()]

        def expect(name, got, want_rc, *want_lines):
            rc, out = got
            n[0] += 1
            lines = out.split("\n")
            if rc != want_rc:
                print("FAIL %s: exit %d, expected %d" % (name, rc, want_rc))
                print("    " + "\n    ".join(lines))
                return False
            for w in want_lines:
                if w not in lines:
                    print("FAIL %s: no line %r" % (name, w))
                    print("    " + "\n    ".join(lines))
                    return False
            if os.path.exists(os.path.join(S, "unscripted")):
                print("FAIL %s: the stand-in was called for something no case scripted" % name)
                print("    " + "\n    ".join(lines))
                return False
            print("ok   %s" % name)
            return True

        def absent(name, got, s):
            if s in got[1]:
                print("FAIL %s: %r stands there and should not" % (name, s))
                return False
            return True

        def rule_stands():
            r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, RULE, "write"], capture_output=True,
                               text=True, env=env)
            assert r.returncode == 0, r.stdout + r.stderr

        def rule_unreadable():
            with open(settings, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.write("{ not json\n")

        ok = True
        lit = "brew install gitleaks"
        lit2 = "npx playwright install chromium"
        # the arguments
        ok &= expect("an argument it does not know: exit 2", run("--nope"), 2,
                     "devloop-auto-mode-question: expected a project directory, "
                     "record WAY ID [DIR] with the command on stdin, or --self-test, got --nope")
        ok &= expect("two directories: exit 2", run("a", "b"), 2,
                     "devloop-auto-mode-question: expected at most one directory, got 2")
        ok &= expect("record with one argument: exit 2", run("record", "brew install"), 2,
                     "devloop-auto-mode-question: record expects WAY and ID, then at most one directory")
        # record
        fresh()
        ok &= expect("record: the line written under .git, exit 0",
                     run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n"), 0,
                     "recorded in %s: brew install" % denials_file)
        with open(denials_file, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            text = f.read()
        parts = text.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
        n[0] += 1
        if len(parts) == 4 and parts[1] == "brew install" and parts[2] == "toolu_1" and parts[3] == lit and parts[0].endswith("Z"):
            print("ok   record: four fields, the time in UTC, the way, the id, the literal")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL record: the line reads %r" % text)
        ok &= expect("record of a second refusal appends a line",
                     run("record", "playwright install", "toolu_2", stdin=lit2 + "\n"), 0,
                     "recorded in %s: playwright install" % denials_file)
        with open(denials_file, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            n[0] += 1
            if len(f.read().rstrip("\n").split("\n")) == 2:
                print("ok   record: two lines stand")
            else:
                ok = False
                print("FAIL record: not two lines")
        ok &= expect("record with a way carrying a tab and a line break: flattened to spaces",
                     run("record", "brew\tinstall\nx", "tool u", stdin=lit + "\n"), 0,
                     "recorded in %s: brew install x" % denials_file)
        ok &= expect("record of a literal that is no JSON string: not recorded, exit 2",
                     run("record", "brew install", "toolu_3", stdin='a "quote\n'), 2,
                     "not recorded: the command on stdin is no JSON string literal")
        ok &= expect("record outside a git repository: not recorded, exit 2",
                     run("record", "brew install", "toolu_3", os.path.join(T, "nogit"), stdin=lit + "\n"), 2)
        n[0] += 1
        if "not recorded: %s is no git repository" % os.path.join(T, "nogit") in run("record", "brew install", "t", os.path.join(T, "nogit"), stdin=lit + "\n")[1]:
            print("ok   record outside a git repository names the directory")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL record outside a git repository does not name the directory")
        # the question: the rule stands
        fresh(); rule_stands()
        ok &= expect("the rule stands: no question, gh not called, exit 1", run(), 1,
                     "question: no", "read: 0")
        n[0] += 1
        if calls() == []:
            print("ok   the rule stands: gh not called")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL the rule stands: gh called: %r" % calls())
        # the rule missing, nothing recorded
        fresh()
        ok &= expect("the rule missing and no refusal recorded: no question, exit 1", run(), 1,
                     "question: no", "read: 1", "denials: none recorded")
        n[0] += 1
        if calls() == []:
            print("ok   nothing recorded: gh not called")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL nothing recorded: gh called: %r" % calls())
        # recorded, no open issue carrying it
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((5, "Something else", "no install here"))
        ok &= expect("a refusal recorded and no open issue carrying the command: no question, exit 1", run(), 1,
                     "question: no", "read: 1",
                     "issues: none of the 1 open issues labelled raised-here and needs-human carries a recorded command")
        # recorded, an issue holding a task
        issues((12, "Install gitleaks for task #7", "Auto mode refused:\n\n    brew install gitleaks\n\nat ~/bin nothing stands."))
        blocking(12, "open")
        open(os.path.join(S, "log"), "w").close()
        ok &= expect("the rule missing and an open issue holding a task: question, kind task, exit 0", run(), 0,
                     "question: yes", "read: 1", "issue: 12", "kind: task",
                     "title: Install gitleaks for task #7", "way: brew install", "command: brew install gitleaks")
        n[0] += 1
        if calls() == ["issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
                       "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/12/dependencies/blocking"]:
            print("ok   the issue list read once and the blocking relation of the one issue")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL the calls were %r" % calls())
        # an issue concerning a check class
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class secrets: auto mode refused the install", "`brew install gitleaks`"))
        blocking(15)
        ok &= expect("the rule missing and an open issue concerning a check class: question, kind class", run(), 0,
                     "question: yes", "issue: 15", "kind: class", "way: brew install")
        # the blocked task closed: no open issue is blocked, kind class
        blocking(15, "closed")
        ok &= expect("an issue whose blocked task is closed: kind class", run(), 0, "issue: 15", "kind: class")
        # both kinds open
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n"); run("record", "playwright install", "toolu_2", stdin=lit2 + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "`brew install gitleaks`"), (12, "The task", "npx playwright install chromium\n"), (3, "Other", "nothing"))
        blocking(12, "closed", "open"); blocking(15)
        got = run()
        ok &= expect("both kinds open: question naming both, in the order of their numbers", got, 0,
                     "question: yes", "issue: 12", "kind: task", "way: playwright install",
                     "command: npx playwright install chromium", "issue: 15", "kind: class")
        n[0] += 1
        if got[1].index("issue: 12") < got[1].index("issue: 15"):
            print("ok   both kinds open: the lower number first")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL both kinds open: the order is wrong")
        # the same command in two issues: both named, each once
        issues((15, "The class", "`brew install gitleaks`"), (16, "Again", "brew install gitleaks"))
        blocking(15); blocking(16)
        got = run()
        ok &= expect("one command carried by two open issues: both named", got, 0, "issue: 15", "issue: 16")
        n[0] += 1
        if got[1].count("issue: 15") == 1:
            print("ok   one command carried by two open issues: each once")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL an issue named twice")
        # read ends with 2
        fresh(); rule_unreadable(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks")); blocking(15)
        ok &= expect("the settings unreadable, read 2, and an open issue: question, read: 2", run(), 0,
                     "question: yes", "read: 2", "issue: 15")
        # the issue closed
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues()
        ok &= expect("the issue closed, the list empty: no question, exit 1", run(), 1,
                     "question: no", "issues: none of the 0 open issues labelled raised-here and needs-human carries a recorded command")
        # after a no, the issue still open: the question again, nothing written
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks")); blocking(15)
        before = open(denials_file, encoding="utf-8").read()
        ok &= expect("after a no the issue still open: the question again", run(), 0, "question: yes", "issue: 15")
        ok &= expect("and again", run(), 0, "question: yes", "issue: 15")
        n[0] += 1
        if open(denials_file, encoding="utf-8").read() == before and not os.path.exists(settings):
            print("ok   the question writes nothing: the record and the settings as before")
        else:
            ok = False
            print("FAIL the question wrote something")
        # a command carried in the title alone
        issues((15, "brew install gitleaks refused", "")); blocking(15)
        ok &= expect("the command in the title alone: found", run(), 0, "issue: 15")
        # a command carried otherwise: not found
        issues((15, "The class", "brew  install gitleaks"))
        ok &= expect("the command carried otherwise, two blanks: not found, no question", run(), 1, "question: no")
        # a multi-line command
        fresh(); run("record", "cargo install", "toolu_9", stdin="cargo install \\\\\\n  ripgrep\n")
        issues((20, "Task", "    cargo install \\\n  ripgrep\n")); blocking(20, "open")
        ok &= expect("a command over two lines: matched verbatim, printed on one line", run(), 0,
                     "issue: 20", "command: cargo install \\   ripgrep")
        # a line of the record not in the form
        fresh()
        with open(denials_file, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write("garbage\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks"))
        ok &= expect("a record whose lines are not in the form: as none recorded", run(), 1, "denials: none recorded")
        # gh does not answer
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        ans("issues", 1, "", "HTTP 502: Bad Gateway (https://api.github.com/graphql)")
        ok &= expect("gh issue list fails: not decided, the command and the message, exit 2", run(), 2,
                     "question: not decided",
                     "command: gh issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
                     "message: HTTP 502: Bad Gateway (https://api.github.com/graphql)")
        ans("issues", 0, "not json")
        ok &= expect("gh prints no JSON: not decided", run(), 2, "question: not decided")
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks"))
        ans("blocking.15", 1, "", "HTTP 404: Not Found")
        ok &= expect("the blocking relation does not answer: not decided", run(), 2,
                     "question: not decided", "command: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/15/dependencies/blocking",
                     "message: HTTP 404: Not Found")
        # a project that is no git repository
        ok &= expect("a directory that is no git repository: no question", run(os.path.join(T, "nogit")), 1,
                     "question: no")
        # CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR
        fresh(); run("record", "brew install", "toolu_1", stdin=lit + "\n")
        issues((15, "The class", "brew install gitleaks")); blocking(15)
        env2 = dict(env); env2["CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR"] = os.path.join(T, "p")
        r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me], cwd=T, capture_output=True, text=True, env=env2)
        ok &= expect("the project from CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR where no directory is given", (r.returncode, r.stdout + r.stderr), 0, "issue: 15")
        if ok:
            print("SELF-TEST PASSED: %d cases" % n[0])
            return 0
        print("SELF-TEST FAILED")
        return 1
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(T, ignore_errors=True)


def main(argv):
    args = argv[1:]
    if args == ["--self-test"]:
        return selftest()
    if args and args[0] == "record":
        rest = args[1:]
        if len(rest) < 2 or len(rest) > 3:
            return usage("record expects WAY and ID, then at most one directory")
        return record(rest[0], rest[1], project_dir(rest[2] if len(rest) == 3 else ""))
    dirs = [a for a in args if not a.startswith("-")]
    bad = [a for a in args if a.startswith("-")]
    if bad:
        return usage("expected a project directory, record WAY ID [DIR] with the "
                     "command on stdin, or --self-test, got %s" % bad[0])
    if len(dirs) > 1:
        return usage("expected at most one directory, got %d" % len(dirs))
    return question(project_dir(dirs[0] if dirs else ""))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
